--- a/apps/reax/docs/operations.md
+++ b/apps/reax/docs/operations.md
@@ -533,6 +533,64 @@ Result includes:
 
 Ledger rows: `rea.tool/inspect_byte_entropy`.
 
+### `read.disassembly`
+
+The instructions of one procedure (by name, or the one holding an address): address, bytes, mnemonic and operands in Intel syntax, from llvm-objdump run once per target in the sandbox and cached. Boundaries are reax's own procedure table; every instruction is checked against the file's bytes. Pages with offset and limit. Linux and macOS (Windows: refused until engines are sandboxed there).
+
+Effect: read. Cost: deep. Output from the target is untrusted (sent inside the untrusted-content envelope). Platforms: linux, darwin.
+
+| Argument | Type | Required | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `path` | string (≤ 4096 chars) | yes |  | File path inside the workspace |
+| `name` | string (≤ 4096 chars) | no |  | A procedure's exact name |
+| `address` | string (≤ 24 chars) | no |  | An address inside the procedure, hex ("0x...") or decimal |
+| `arch` | string (≤ 32 chars) | no |  | The file's architecture (a thin file has one; universal binaries are not disassembled yet) |
+| `offset` | integer (≥ 0) | no | `0` | first row to return (page.next continues) |
+| `limit` | integer (≥ 1, ≤ 10000) | no | `200` | rows per page |
+
+Example: The instructions of main in a C++ executable.
+
+```json
+{"operation":"read.disassembly","path":"shapes-x86_64-linux-gnu.2.28-g","name":"main"}
+```
+
+Result includes:
+
+```json
+{"procedure":{"name":"main","addr":"0x10015b0"},"items":[{"addr":"0x10015b0","bytes":"55","mnemonic":"push","operands":"rbp"}]}
+```
+
+Ledger rows: `rea.tool/procedure_assembly`, `rea.tool/read_function_instructions`.
+
+### `search.disassembly`
+
+Search a target's whole disassembly (dumped once and cached, as read.disassembly): instructions whose mnemonic is exactly the one given (LLVM's spelling, e.g. call), or whose mnemonic and operands contain a text (case-insensitive, no regular expressions). Each hit names its procedure, or null where no sized procedure holds it. Pages with offset and limit. Linux and macOS.
+
+Effect: read. Cost: deep. Output from the target is untrusted (sent inside the untrusted-content envelope). Platforms: linux, darwin.
+
+| Argument | Type | Required | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `path` | string (≤ 4096 chars) | yes |  | File path inside the workspace |
+| `arch` | string (≤ 32 chars) | no |  | The file's architecture (a thin file has one; universal binaries are not disassembled yet) |
+| `mnemonic` | string (≤ 64 chars) | no |  | An exact mnemonic, e.g. call |
+| `text` | string (≤ 256 chars) | no |  | Text the mnemonic and operands contain (any case) |
+| `offset` | integer (≥ 0) | no | `0` | first row to return (page.next continues) |
+| `limit` | integer (≥ 1, ≤ 10000) | no | `200` | rows per page |
+
+Example: Every call in a small C executable.
+
+```json
+{"operation":"search.disassembly","path":"hello-x86_64-linux-gnu.2.28-g","mnemonic":"call"}
+```
+
+Result includes:
+
+```json
+{"items":[{"mnemonic":"call","procedure":{"name":"_start"}}]}
+```
+
+Ledger rows: `leanrea/LEAN-C08`.
+
 ### `search.symbols`
 
 Read back the symbols session.index stored for a target (by path or digest), paged in the reader's order; with query, only those whose names contain the text (case-insensitive, 3 to 256 characters, matched literally; a query containing NUL is refused).
